WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.400
When you catabolize an amino acid, you face two separate problems.

2
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:13.660
The carbon skeleton is valuable fuel that can be burned in the TCA cycle or rebuilt

3
00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:18.360
into glucose and fatty acids, but the amino nitrogen is a liability.

4
00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:22.840
Free ammonia is toxic, so the cell splits the molecule.

5
00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:28.420
Nitrogen is collected onto glutamate, shuttled safely through the blood as alanine

6
00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:34.000
and glutamine, funneled to the liver, run through the urea cycle, and excreted in

7
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.840
urine. Carbon can be burned or rebuilt.

8
00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:41.000
Nitrogen must be collected, transported safely, and excreted.

9
00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:45.340
Keeping these two fates separate is the organizing logic of the entire chapter.

10
00:00:46.283 --> 00:00:49.523
Stripping nitrogen from an amino acid takes two moves.

11
00:00:50.203 --> 00:00:51.763
Step one is transamination.

12
00:00:52.383 --> 00:00:57.183
An amino transferase hands the amino group from many different amino acids onto

13
00:00:57.183 --> 00:01:02.163
alpha-ketoglutarate, collecting all that nitrogen onto a single carrier, glutamate.

14
00:01:02.763 --> 00:01:06.823
This step is reversible and funnels diverse inputs into one channel.

15
00:01:07.503 --> 00:01:09.623
Step two is oxidative deamination.

16
00:01:15.583 --> 00:01:17.703
Glutamate to do it all again.

17
00:01:18.283 --> 00:01:22.643
The reaction can run both ways, but in the liver the urea cycle keeps drawing the

18
00:01:22.643 --> 00:01:25.663
ammonia off, so the nitrogen flows toward disposal.

19
00:01:26.383 --> 00:01:31.423
Many amino acids in, two moves, and free ammonia out, ready to be packaged safely

20
00:01:31.423 --> 00:01:32.483
for the trip to the liver.

21
00:01:33.317 --> 00:01:36.377
Working muscle has a clever way to ship its nitrogen home.

22
00:01:36.917 --> 00:01:41.797
As muscle breaks down amino acids, it transaminates the nitrogen onto pyruvate to

23
00:01:41.797 --> 00:01:46.837
make alanine. Alanine is non-toxic, so it travels safely through the blood to the

24
00:01:46.837 --> 00:01:51.617
liver. There the liver strips the nitrogen back off, feeding it into the urea cycle,

25
00:01:52.037 --> 00:01:54.637
and converts the leftover pyruvate into glucose.

26
00:01:55.377 --> 00:01:57.817
That glucose returns to the muscle as fuel.

27
00:01:58.397 --> 00:02:02.477
So alanine is the nitrogen shuttle, and glucose is the energy shuttle.

28
00:02:02.477 --> 00:02:04.197
Running in opposite directions.

29
00:02:05.097 --> 00:02:09.677
The muscle offloads its waste nitrogen and gets fuel back in the same elegant round

30
00:02:09.677 --> 00:02:10.677
trip.

31
00:02:10.817 --> 00:02:15.717
Glutamine is the workhorse of nitrogen transport and the most abundant free amino

32
00:02:15.717 --> 00:02:16.877
acid in your blood.

33
00:02:17.497 --> 00:02:22.217
Tissues everywhere add a second amino group onto glutamate to make glutamine, which

34
00:02:22.217 --> 00:02:25.037
carries two nitrogens safely and without toxicity.

35
00:02:25.797 --> 00:02:28.177
The bloodstream then delivers it where it is needed.

36
00:02:28.617 --> 00:02:33.457
The liver for urea synthesis, the kidney for acid-base balance, and rapidly

37
00:02:33.457 --> 00:02:38.317
dividing cells like those in the gut and immune system that use glutamine as fuel

38
00:02:38.317 --> 00:02:39.637
and as a building block.

39
00:02:40.157 --> 00:02:43.397
Ammonia is toxic, so the body almost never moves it freely.

40
00:02:44.097 --> 00:02:48.777
Most nitrogen travels as glutamine, the safe, abundant, two-nitrogen courier.

41
00:02:49.667 --> 00:02:52.807
Here is an analogy that makes the liver's chemistry click.

42
00:02:53.527 --> 00:02:56.507
Picture a bicycle with two wheels that share a chain.

43
00:02:57.087 --> 00:03:02.627
One wheel is the urea cycle, the other is the TCA cycle, and they are physically

44
00:03:02.627 --> 00:03:06.287
linked through shared intermediates like fumarate and aspartate.

45
00:03:06.767 --> 00:03:08.607
When you pedal, both wheels turn together.

46
00:03:09.227 --> 00:03:13.687
Urea comes out one side as the nitrogen waste product, and glucose-building carbon

47
00:03:13.687 --> 00:03:14.587
comes out the other.

48
00:03:14.587 --> 00:03:17.007
The liver runs both wheels at once.

49
00:03:17.507 --> 00:03:21.567
Dispose of nitrogen as urea while reclaiming carbon for energy and glucose.

50
00:03:22.227 --> 00:03:26.907
Nitrogen out one side, glucose out the other, one connected machine doing two jobs.
